      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\tan V\\) in the given right triangle \\(VWX\\), we need to first identify the sides of the triangle. We are given that \\(VW = 3\\) and \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\). Since \\(VWX\\) is a right triangle with \\(\\angle WXV = 90^\\circ\\), we can label the sides as follows: \\(VW\\) is the opposite side to \\(\\angle V\\), \\(XV\\) is the adjacent side to \\(\\angle V\\), and \\(WX\\) is the hypotenuse.\n\nWe need to find the length of \\(XV\\) (the adjacent side to \\(\\angle V\\)). By the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 13 - 9\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 4\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nWX = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\tan V\\). The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. For \\(\\angle V\\), the opposite side is \\(VW\\) and the adjacent side is \\(XV\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{VW}{XV} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]",
